# Line endings, pinned so they cannot drift. The repository is read and edited on Windows and
# built on Linux, and before this file the two had quietly disagreed: 130 of the 333 tracked
# files were CRLF and 188 were LF, with no rule behind either (a `cargo fmt` run once rewrote a
# four-line edit as a 450-line file because the file it touched was CRLF).
#
# One rule: LF everywhere. That is what the image runs — anything executed inside the container
# (Build/docker/*.sh, Build/tests/*.sh, Build/tmuxp/*.yaml) fails on CRLF with a `\r` error that
# reads like a missing file — and it is what every tool here writes. `git ls-files --eol` is the
# check; a fresh clone should report LF for everything except the two exceptions below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Exception 1 — the vendored interpreter. dFrotzTooled is upstream Frotz 2.54 plus one patch,
# and VENDOR.md documents where it came from; its bytes are its provenance, so we do not
# normalise them even though nothing would break if we did.
Source/dFrotzTooled/** -text

# Exception 2 — the game sidecars. These are prose written at a Windows keyboard, so they are
# checked out with the line endings that keyboard produces. They are still stored as LF like
# everything else, and nothing but their checkout is different: Documents/02_game_boundary.md §4.4
# describes what goes in them.
Assets/games/*.md text eol=crlf

# Exception 3 — the rendered documents. PDFs are bytes: `text=auto` sniffs them as binary anyway,
# and this says so rather than leaving the checkout dependent on a sniff. `Build/docs-pdf.sh`
# regenerates them from the markdown, which is the source of truth.
Documents/pdf/** -text

# What the public release leaves behind. `Build/publish.sh` builds the release tree with
# `git archive`, which drops every path marked `export-ignore` — so this line, and not a list of
# paths inside a script, is what says the design set is private. It also keeps the set and its
# rendered PDFs out of GitHub's own archive download of any tag, which is the other way the
# whole directory would otherwise travel.
Documents/ export-ignore

# And the publisher itself: it cuts the release, it is not part of one. In the public tree it
# could only describe a repository the reader cannot see.
Build/publish.sh export-ignore

# Internal working notes. They name files a reader of the release tree will not have, and they
# are a record of one evening's testing rather than documentation of the software.
Build/uat-findings-20260929.md export-ignore
Build/uat-runbook.md export-ignore

# The maintainer's half of the README: architecture, the full configuration and command
# reference, and how the thing is built and tested. The public README is the short one.
README-private.md export-ignore
